 is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=A{e}^{2x}\\,+\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,+\\,($\n{{field-2}}\n$)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2c887c244ace8e272d9f1089ed6014f2ecd5958fae1699b208fd7a9c8c6559d"} {"question" "q-332875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n5\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-10{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n5(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x))-10(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(20\\beta -10\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+(-20\\alpha -10\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}20\\beta -10\\alpha =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)) \\\\ -20\\alpha -10\\beta =-7\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{11}{50},\\beta =\\frac{13}{50}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{11}{50}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+\\frac{13}{50}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+(\\frac{11}{50})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+(\\frac{13}{50})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2c887c244ace8e272d9f1089ed6014f2ecd5958fae1699b208fd7a9c8c6559d"} {"question" "q-332875" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12ed4c1945989d306c156c91b9d2d03f45f80cd5294516e422b43681fcf32248"} {"question" "q-332875" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2c887c244ace8e272d9f1089ed6014f2ecd5958fae1699b208fd7a9c8c6559d"} {"question" "q-332875" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{11}{50}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2c887c244ace8e272d9f1089ed6014f2ecd5958fae1699b208fd7a9c8c6559d"} {"question" "q-332875" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2c887c244ace8e272d9f1089ed6014f2ecd5958fae1699b208fd7a9c8c6559d"} {"question" "q-332875" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{13}{50}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2c887c244ace8e272d9f1089ed6014f2ecd5958fae1699b208fd7a9c8c6559d"} {"question" "q-333396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x),\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2c887c244ace8e272d9f1089ed6014f2ecd5958fae1699b208fd7a9c8c6559d"} {"question" "q-333396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))+4(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(3\\beta +4\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+(-3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3\\beta +4\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -3\\alpha +4\\beta =25\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-3,\\beta =4.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-4\\cdot 0}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) & =2 \\\\ A-3-0 & =2 \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=5{e}^{-4x}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2c887c244ace8e272d9f1089ed6014f2ecd5958fae1699b208fd7a9c8c6559d"} {"question" "q-333396" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12ed4c1945989d306c156c91b9d2d03f45f80cd5294516e422b43681fcf32248"} {"question" "q-333396" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2c887c244ace8e272d9f1089ed6014f2ecd5958fae1699b208fd7a9c8c6559d"} {"question" "q-333396" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5e^{-4x}-3\\cos(3x)+4\\sin(3x)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2c887c244ace8e272d9f1089ed6014f2ecd5958fae1699b208fd7a9c8c6559d"} {"question" "q-332999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=13\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,y(0)=5\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=13\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2c887c244ace8e272d9f1089ed6014f2ecd5958fae1699b208fd7a9c8c6559d"} {"question" "q-332999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c}$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $13\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+3(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=13\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\beta +3\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-2\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=13\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta +3\\alpha =13\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -2\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =3,\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=5$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-3\\cdot 0}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & =5 \\\\ A+3+0 & =5 \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=2{e}^{-3x}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2c887c244ace8e272d9f1089ed6014f2ecd5958fae1699b208fd7a9c8c6559d"} {"question" "q-332999" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12ed4c1945989d306c156c91b9d2d03f45f80cd5294516e422b43681fcf32248"} {"question" "q-332999" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2c887c244ace8e272d9f1089ed6014f2ecd5958fae1699b208fd7a9c8c6559d"} {"question" "q-332999" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2e^{-3x}+3\\cos(2x)+2\\sin(2x)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2c887c244ace8e272d9f1089ed6014f2ecd5958fae1699b208fd7a9c8c6559d"} {"question" "q-332973" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=13\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}