),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))-(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\beta -\\alpha )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+(-2\\alpha -\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta -\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -2\\alpha -\\beta =10\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-4,\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{1\\cdot 0}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & =3 \\\\ A-4-0 & =3 \\\\ A & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=7{e}^{x}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2c887c244ace8e272d9f1089ed6014f2ecd5958fae1699b208fd7a9c8c6559d"} {"question" "q-332972" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12ed4c1945989d306c156c91b9d2d03f45f80cd5294516e422b43681fcf32248"} {"question" "q-332972" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2c887c244ace8e272d9f1089ed6014f2ecd5958fae1699b208fd7a9c8c6559d"} {"question" "q-332972" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7e^{x}-4\\cos(2x)-2\\sin(2x)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2c887c244ace8e272d9f1089ed6014f2ecd5958fae1699b208fd7a9c8c6559d"} {"question" "e-21618" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} + 3 y = 2\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) =  A e ^ {-3x}$, find the particular solution $y_p(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/example-21618.json" "file_sha256" "d1b1a529bb0afe595f6bcfda3d8bfecf741f1abf2f4bb4e4b552d727f0a549c9"} {"question" "e-21618" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $2\\cos{x}.$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\cos x + \\beta  \\sin x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} =   - \\alpha \\sin {x} +  \\beta \\cos {x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n( - \\alpha \\sin {x} +  \\beta \\cos {x}) + 3(\\alpha \\cos x + \\beta  \\sin x) = 2\\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(3\\alpha+\\beta)\\cos x + (-\\alpha + 3\\beta) \\sin x = 2\\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha +\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\ -\\alpha +3\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha = \\dfrac 3 5,$ $\\beta = \\dfrac 1 5.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\dfrac{3}{5} \\cos(x) + \\dfrac{1}{5} \\sin(x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/example-21618.json" "file_sha256" "d1b1a529bb0afe595f6bcfda3d8bfecf741f1abf2f4bb4e4b552d727f0a549c9"} {"question" "e-21619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 4 y = 8 \\cos(3x) - 2 \\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_c(x) = Ae^{-2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/example-21619.json" "file_sha256" "9fe0fad9bd8cde0629c6a081b332461d0b737df738493d742eee82996abb6ff3"} {"question" "e-21619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\textrm{d} y_c}{\\textrm{d} x} + 4 y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $8\\cos(3x) - 2\\sin(3x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\cos(3x) + \\beta  \\sin(3x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} =   - 3\\alpha \\sin(3x) +  3\\beta \\cos(3x).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2 ( - 3\\alpha \\sin(3x) +  3\\beta \\cos(3x)) + 4(\\alpha \\cos(3x)+ \\beta  \\sin(3x)) = 8 \\cos(3x) - 2\\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(6\\beta + 4\\alpha)\\cos(3x) + (-6\\alpha + 4 \\beta) \\sin(3x) = 8 \\cos(3x) - 2\\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6\\beta +4\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)) \\\\ -6\\alpha +4\\beta =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=\\dfrac{11}{13}, \\beta=\\dfrac{10}{13}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\ny_p(x) =  \\dfrac{11}{13}\\cos(3x) + \\dfrac{10}{13} \\sin(3x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny =  A e^{-2x} + \\left(\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{11}{13} }\\right)\\cos(3x) \n+ \n\\left(\\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{10}{13} }\\right) \\sin(3x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/example-21619.json" "file_sha256" "9fe0fad9bd8cde0629c6a081b332461d0b737df738493d742eee82996abb6ff3"} {"question" "e-21620" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' + 3y = 26 \\sin(2x), \\qquad y(0) =  1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' + 3y = 26 \\sin(2x)\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c = Ae^{-3x},$ solve the initial value problem." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/example-21620.json" "file_sha256" "790ca0a21ddb05db4c984da4c263e1463295c8f365bc96ca9fff015897955c1d"} {"question" "e-21620" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' + 3y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is $26 \\sin(2x).$ So, we assume the particular solution to be \n\n$$\ny_p(x) = \\alpha \\cos(2x) + \\beta  \\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\ny_p' =   - 2\\alpha \\sin(2x) +  2\\beta \\cos(2x)\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha,$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n( - 2\\alpha \\sin(2x) +  2\\beta \\cos(2x)) + 3 (\\alpha \\cos(2x)+ \\beta  \\sin(2x)) = 26 \\sin(2x)\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n(2\\beta+3\\alpha)\\cos(2x) + (-2\\alpha +3 \\beta) \\sin(2x) = 26 \\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\beta +3\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)) \\\\ -2\\alpha +3\\beta =26\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-4, \\beta=6.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) =  - 4\\cos(2x) + 6\\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny =  Ae^{-3x} - 4\\cos(2x) + 6\\sin(2x).\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0) =1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-3\\cdot 0}-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & =1 \\\\ A-4+0 & =1 \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 5e^{-3x} - 4\\cos(2x) + 6\\sin(2x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14073319/example-21620.json" "file_sha256" "790ca0a21ddb05db4c984da4c263e1463295c8f365bc96ca9fff015897955c1d"}] "usage" {"q-332972" [] "q-332676" [] "q-332973" [] "q-332623" [] "q-333396" [] "q-332999" [] "e-21618" [] "q-332886" [] "e-21619" [] "e-21620" [] "q-332793" [] "q-332754" []} "basis" 2695 "retractions" [[17592186229595 :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + 2y = 7\\cos x$\nhas the complementary solution $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{-2x}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation.$y_{p}(x) =$\n{{field-1}}\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"finding-the-general-solution-to-a-first-orde